h  seeing the same sort of trend there but  but obviously on this coarse soil  um we're seeing more of a response to  nitrogen than we are on the  on the uh the lumiere ground so so um  here too  we don't see a real response on the  loams when we get into  the sandier soils there seems to be more  of a response because likely the  just the lack of of or the lower organic  matter the  the higher rate of of um  of leaching if it's there so not  completely unexpected  okay moving on to micronutrients um  a lot of a lot of people try to to sell  sell you micronutrients um my philosophy  on that is unless you know you've got a  problem  if your soil is well maintained if your  ph is good if you've got good  good healthy production practices i  don't really  see a use in that um  my suggestion which i've heard from  others is is if you want to get a  baseline  of what micronutrients should be in your  crop we do have recommendations in  ontario  of what the the level of nutri nutrients  in the plant should be  based on tissue samples but if you want  to know what it's like on your  own site um go to an area on your farm  where you've got good production where  you're happy with with how the plants  performing and they look healthy  take some tissue samples send them to  the lab kind of get a baseline of of  what is in your plants at that  particular location and then if you run  into trouble  you can compare them to that you can see  where you're you're getting  getting into trouble and and may explain  some problems that you're seeing if  there are in fact micronutrient  deficiencies  there are suggestions in the literature  of high boron requirements uh we've not  seen that on our sites um we spend a lot  of time  on uh the only one that i'm really  familiar with is is um  is iron and becky hughes who works for  the university of guelph and our in a  northern location  regularly um adds foliar iron to her day  neutrals because of uh  because of a deficiency she's got there  on the clay but certainly not something  that we've seen  where we are where we are in  in our site um this is a a series of of  samples that i took um early on when we  started working with day neutrals  weren't really familiar with them  this is the the second year crop so this  is the year after planting this is a  seascape crop  at our cedar springs site we talk off  about 19.5 tons per hectare on on this  particular planting and we refer to  fertigating with a complete blend triple  20 and putting on about  0.6 kilo per hectare per day and we  would put them on once a week  and so um we were we were seeing some  symptoms under our tunnels this is the  outside  production where i was taking the  samples from but we were seeing some  some symptoms that we weren't really  sure uh were were  we weren't really sure what was going on  and so the concern was micronutrients so  we started  sampling and we tried to get in every  two weeks and and take fully expanded  leaves recently expanded leaves so  they're fairly young and  analyzing the petioles of them and uh in  the second column that's the recommended  rates in ontario you can see the dates  as we go across what our nutrient status  was and  the red the red numbers  indicate where we were falling below the  ontario  recommended rates and you can see  there's a few cases where  nitrogen was dipping a little bit but  certainly we weren't wildly low  in our rates um potash again a little a  few times  mid-season where we were getting below  the recommended rates but but  uh not moving out in any any great  degree so  so um it really wasn't anything to do  with nutrition and then we realized now  that was more heat stress on the plants  than anything but  um it was just a nice way to to kind of  see  where we're going what our nutrient  status was in particular the micros and  and uh just managing them without  putting any great effort on micro  we're seeing any big problems there and  no big differences  so just some general comments to  summarize the fertigation topic  if you're new to it uh start simple  become comfortable with your irrigation  system and your  your fertigation systems again  uniformity is is important in putting  the water down and then when the  fertility is carried along with it  hopefully that will will follow the same  trend  um soil testing and tissue testing are  good indications of what's available in  the soil  and what's getting into the plant and  it'll it'll set up some guidelines for  you and  in all cases make sure your record  keeping is good what products you were  using what rates you were putting on  if you were happy with the condition of  your plants if you think your yield  potential was there  you can always go back and look at those  and they give you a good indication of  what was going on  as you move on you can refine them um  one of the questions i've got is whether  or not  more frequent smaller doses of fertility  are applicable  um kind of thinking in terms of  vegetable transplant production where  where daily applications we find are  more efficient and easier to do than say  weekly applications  um applications based on on plant growth  is something i think that is up and  coming and and might be interesting but  we  don't think we really have the  information on that yet uh differences  in variety um  we wonder if albion just needs a little  bit more to push it along because it  tends to be a little slower maturing as  simon mentioned but  but also it tends to we think need a  little bit more  in terms of uh of growing the plant  and then just sap testing on the farm  there are a number of um  instruments where you can go into the  field yourself and then test for  different nutrients and  and whether that could be part of a  fertigation program just to  try and maintain the quality and the  nutrient status of the plant as you go  through the season  okay that kind of concludes what uh what  i wanted to talk about i just got a list  of some of the  books and uh yeah the books that i used  to put this together and  and there were a number of research  articles as well that that we looked at  so  so if you want to look at them further  so thank you for your time and uh  yeah okay  okay john thank you very much there are  some questions in the chat box  uh the first one is from rajmiki again  he's wondering if the application of  multi-microbial inocula as a bio  fertilizer  could be more effective in plants growth  promotion than humic acid application  oh i really have no experience with  any of that um  um yeah i've not at all worked in that  and i really didn't focus on that when i  was reviewing the literature so  sorry i i really don't know what to say  in that respect it's certainly an  interesting area looking at  biofertilizers but  it isn't something that i focused on in  in terms of this presentation  okay rod elmstrand would like to know uh  why white plastic or black we're in a  fairly warm production area so we prefer  white it um it doesn't give us a good  head start in the season but when we get  into the warmer part of the summers  we notice a difference on on in terms of  production and quality so white is  better black just gets too warm  it just isn't a good situation for us we  want to we want to be able to cool the  plants or grow them in a cooler  environment  i was just joking with a plastic  supplier a few days ago ideally it would  be nice  we would have something that was black  in the summer and then started to fade  and and turn white later in the year but  um the thought has been about uh  white washing and we've looked at these  skunk-type plastics as well  but the basic reason is is we're warm we  want a cooler environment for our plants  so so we use a white plastic  kay is wondering if there's any other  research with white plastic around the  country or the world so if any of our  other participants  have some comments on that please feel  free to 